  <p><em>Translated from the Chinese original.</em></p>

  <p>Traditional apps are designed for people. Screens, buttons, feeds, paywalls: the whole interface assumes that a user will look, tap, and stay. The business model followed naturally. On the user side, the product is free or nearly free, and the real levy is an attention tax. On the merchant side, the platform takes a cut, folding channel costs into every transaction.</p>

  <p>The problem is that attention is changing hands.</p>

  <p>As users outsource more comparison, checkout, and after-sales work to a local or cloud agent, the old extraction machine starts to jam. An agent does not scroll short videos and does not get hooked on red dots. It wants callable capabilities, verifiable results, and a bill it can settle. The real tension today may not be whether AI will replace apps. It is this: <strong>a new attention paradigm is already shifting, while the old attention-harvesting machinery is still running.</strong></p>

  <h2 id="built-for-people-or-built-for-agents">Built for people, or built for agents</h2>

  <p>If the next generation of applications is no longer built first for people, but for agents, the design target moves from retention, time-on-app, and conversion to interfaces, permissions, billing, and fulfillment. People remain the source of demand. They stop being the traffic entrance that gets intercepted again and again.</p>

  <p>A cleaner chain might look like this:</p>

  <p>user → local / cloud agent ⇄ <strong>service</strong> ⇄ merchant agent → goods and service providers</p>

  <p>The middle layer does not have to be another super app. It can be a trading system that matches, quotes, and fulfills automatically—an automated market maker, in a sense. It would not earn the user’s time. It would earn a toll on traffic: let agents on both sides transact at the lowest possible channel cost, leave the margin with manufacturers and brands, and produce a positive brand effect plus an upgrade in supply.</p>

  <p>That requires a lower-level billing primitive. HTTP 402 Payment Required is being discussed again because it writes “pay on call” back into the protocol: a request can carry payment intent, a response can demand payment, and machines do not have to detour through a human subscription page or an ad slot. Traffic can then be settled per call, per quality, or per outcome, instead of being harvested by dwell time.</p>

  <h2 id="questions-that-are-still-open">Questions that are still open</h2>

  <p>This paradigm sounds like shrinking the platform from an attention casino into a pipe. Pipes grow into power too. At least five questions are worth asking first.</p>

  <h3 id="how-do-small-providers-avoid-being-wiped-out">How do small providers avoid being wiped out</h3>

  <p>If scale costs cannot be compressed, automated matching will see only the cheapest quote, the fastest response, and the most standardized API. Small and micro providers survive only if differentiation and personalization become agent-readable: craft, origin, lead time, repair, small-batch customization—not a single price field. If the platform optimizes only for closing the deal, personalization will be filtered out as noise.</p>

  <h3 id="will-bad-supply-drive-out-good-supply">Will bad supply drive out good supply</h3>

  <p>Agents default to what can be compared, aggregated, and closed. The easiest metrics—price, speed, rating—will crowd out quality that is hard to quantify. Without traceable fulfillment, inspection, and after-the-fact liability, low-grade supply can fill the matching queue with a lower bid. For a toll model to work, billing cannot cover only “number of calls.” It has to price the quality of fulfillment.</p>

  <h3 id="will-one-player-take-the-whole-market">Will one player take the whole market</h3>

  <p>An automated market maker feeds on network density: more supply makes matching sharper, agents are more willing to connect, and more supply then wants in. That is the same flywheel as today’s super platforms. If settlement, identity, credit, and logistics are all bound to one private stack, the toll becomes a new channel tax. Driving channel fees down for real requires a replaceable matching layer and an interoperable protocol layer—not another super app for the agent era.</p>

  <h3 id="is-the-infrastructure-even-ready">Is the infrastructure even ready</h3>

  <p>For this paradigm to run, we need at least: identity and authorization for agents; machine-readable descriptions of goods and services; cross-party payments and clearing (402 is only the doorway); fulfillment and dispute resolution; and user control over the agent. Missing any piece, the chain breaks in reality—not in the demo, but in returns, fraud, privacy, and who is responsible.</p>

  <h3 id="what-each-party-in-the-chain-is-afraid-of">What each party in the chain is afraid of</h3>

  <p>Users fear that the agent will be bought off, and that once data and decision rights are handed over, no one can be held to account. Local and cloud agents fear being cut off by the platform, or having their share drained. The matching service fears a market with no liquidity, and fears being treated as dominant the moment it works. Merchant agents fear comparison shopping flattening a brand into parameters, and fear that opening an API means being scraped dry. Brands and manufacturers fear that even if the toll falls, they lose pricing power, after-sales, and the brand story at the same time. Nobody will stand on the side of a new paradigm just because it is more efficient.</p>

  <h2 id="closing">Closing</h2>

  <p>An agent-facing interaction platform is not a matter of swapping buttons for APIs. It asks whether, once attention no longer belongs to the screen, value exchange can return to a lower-level protocol: a toll instead of an attention tax, matching instead of a commission, and profit returned to the people who make the thing well.</p>

  <p>Whether it becomes a new monopoly, whether it hurts small suppliers, whether it lets bad goods circulate at machine speed, will not be settled by slogans. It will be settled by billing, credit, and interoperability—the arrangements that look like infrastructure. HTTP 402 is only a signal: machines are ready to pay. What is not ready is the kind of market we want them to live in.</p>

